      "body": "## Summary\n\nMapped shared and one-shot channels consume authorized participants' native chat messages during a running turn, with each sender's broker-derived attribution and join line. DMs, private scope, unmapped shared routes and transport ingest retain their existing rules. PR1 retains at most eight participant legs, authorizes overflow text before injection, and keeps scripts on the asker's leg. Handles and the bash `as` argument belong to PR2.\n\nThe final-step stop is resolved under the coordinator's 2026-10-26 ruling: queued messages left after the final model step start later turns, freshly authorized under their own subjects. Folding combines messages only from one subject. Startup mapping resolution promotes only messages admitted during that startup lookup, so older queued turns remain independent. The retained `final_step_leftovers_never_fold_an_unchecked_participant_into_another_subject` regression covers both an authorized third participant and a refused third participant.\n\nPinned GPT.md first line: `# Steering for GPT executors`.\n\n## Security impact\n\nIdentity and authority. A handle is a gateway-minted pointer to a leg whose subject came from the authenticated transport; the model only selects among handles that exist, and the broker authorizes that participant \u2229 space on every frame. Speak-to-spend, default asker and one handle per script bound the residual the design accepts (DESIGN \u00a73): inside a participant's envelope, the model may act on someone else's text under their name.\n\nThat paragraph describes the accepted two-PR design. PR1 supplies participant authorization and attribution; scripts execute on the asker's leg and no handles are emitted. The broker remains the sole authority. Refused or empty participant legs expose no text or assets to the model. Final-step leftovers use the normal fresh-turn authorization/refusal path, never another subject's leg.\n\nOne session-owned async worker serializes authorization. Pending, authorizing and ready messages share the existing 100-item mailbox bound; ready batches retain their reservation through staging. The session owns and joins the worker, retains at most eight legs including the asker, and holds at most one additional temporary overflow leg. All legs share the turn's call budget and cancellation. Worker loss stops the session. Lookup spans carry the asker's turn owner and the joining subject; capability lookups create no broker effect decisions, verified against the real broker with a positive invocation control.\n\n## Validation\n\nHead: `13f5513d1a55f195c0c77a7d07a9ae261d08d9a4`. Rust 1.98.1; cargo-machete 0.9.2. Full required verification is in progress; this is not a claim that all gates or CI passed.\n\n| Head SHA | Gate | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 13f5513d | `git diff --check` | Pass |\n| 13f5513d | `cargo fmt --all --check` | Pass |\n| 13f5513d | clippy | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | release | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | machete | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | boundaries | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | boundary-tests | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | rustdoc | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | fixtures | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | tests | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | doctests | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | deny | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | duplicates | In progress / pending |\n| 13f5513d | audit-docs | In progress / pending |\n\nCI: Runs are active on this exact head: [CI #1780](https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon/actions/runs/40411109153), [WIT package](https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon/actions/runs/40411108820), [memory-lab](https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon/actions/runs/40411108751). Results pending.\n\nAcceptance mapping:\n\n| Acceptance | Test / evidence |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | `mapped_participants_are_consumed_once_with_their_own_attribution_and_join_lines`; `mapping_resolution_promotes_pending_messages_and_keeps_unmapped_routes_queued`; `private_scope_and_direct_messages_keep_other_senders_as_followups`. Final-step leftovers start independently authorized later turns per the coordinator ruling, covered by the retained regression in both authorized/refused cases. |\n| 2 | `refused_and_empty_participant_legs_never_expose_their_messages_or_assets`: no extra model request, later history contains neither refused text nor assets, and one refusal admission record. The final-step regression checks the separate fresh-turn refusal path. |\n| 3 | `overflow_participants_are_authorized_and_refused_before_text_is_injected`: authorized ninth and refused later participant; exactly one overflow admission record. No handles in PR1. |\n| 4 | Attribution test observes participant subject and opening delivery on capability frames. Per-script selection and broker `participant.handle` are PR2 work. |\n| 5 | Explicit/unknown handle selection is PR2 work; PR1 preserves the asker script invoker. |\n| 6 | `only_the_askers_native_chat_steer_interrupts_a_mapped_turn`, plus the model-level mapped-participant test. |\n| 7 | `github_transport_never_steers_even_with_a_native_message_id_on_a_mapped_route`, including the same-subject GitHub control case. |\n| 8 | Gateway attribution test checks lookup parent, `turn.owner` and subject. Real-broker `participant_capability_lookups_do_not_record_effect_decisions` checks two successful capability lookups produce no decision records and a subsequent invocation produces one. |\n| 9 | Exact commands and observed results in the table above. |\n\n## Limitations / follow-ups\n\nPR2 adds handles, the optional `participant` field and the bash `as` argument. Both PRs must ship in one release; hold PR1's drop until PR2 merges. A coordinator-launched Astra review gates PR1; the required Claude review is also pending. PR2 starts after the coordinator's PR1 review and continuation. No merge, tag, release, deployment or main push is authorized.\n\n**Ship order:** PR1 populates the existing optional delivery field and adds no wire field. For PR2, the broker must accept `ChatScopeClaim.participant` before any gateway sends it because `ChatScopeClaim` denies unknown fields. Gateway and broker share one image/pod, so one release satisfies that order. No WIT, SDK or config change.\n\nOther broker.sock clients at the recorded deployment are **dekopon-console 0.58.0** (including embedded TUI) and **dekopon-brokerd probe 0.58.0**. Health/probe, jobs and wakes in **dekopon-gatewayd 0.58.0** are not separately versioned clients. The chart's socket peers are broker/probe, gateway and console; config checks and provider precompile are local commands, not additional socket clients. Evidence: [0.58.0 deployment receipt](https://github.com/dekopon-agents/campaign-trail/blob/main/store/epochs/diaphanous-dilettantes/00-release/dekopon/0.58.0/evidence/DEPLOYED.md), chart 0.45.0, rpi-homelab pin 5fbbf6ce. Gateway, broker and console use image index `sha256:58f1d167f1999e59ad5b269f8057ee9adde56b0dc297d18129939bca0e350028`. Its manifest and actual amd64 sibling binaries were verified (`--version`: 0.58.0 each); no fresh cluster access is claimed.\n\n## Choices I made\n\n- Used exact minimum base 6b47a4aa after finding the cached checkout one commit behind; fetched without tags.\n- Carried Pending/Unmapped/Mapped readiness from the asker's fresh space_grants answer, independently of replay-key selection.\n- Kept startup candidates within the existing mailbox until mapping resolves, then promoted them in arrival order.\n- Used one session-owned async authorization worker and a capacity-one drain request channel from the existing blocking prompt task.\n- Used a bounded vector of authenticated subjects and Arc<BrokerLeg> entries for the eight retained legs, without a second identity index.\n- Kept authorizing/ready batches reserved against the 100-item mailbox through staging using an owned drop guard.\n- Reauthorized overflow messages, closed temporary legs before injection, and retained join tracking only for successful appearances.\n- Reused the shared call budget/cancellation; worker loss fails the session closed.\n- Deferred participant admission records until authorization resolves, retained their receipt trace, and kept lookups under the asker's turn.\n- Derived coarse provider read/write summaries from broker-returned effective capability metadata; added no role field.\n- Filled existing delivery coordinates when constructing native/follow claims; deferred handle emission and model selection to PR2.\n- Used an explicit drain-mode enum and avoided another model request for a refused-only final drain.\n- Recorded the original boundary stop with a failing regression, then followed the coordinator's ruling that final-step leftovers intentionally start later turns under their own subject.\n- Added a subject guard to the existing leftover-folding path and a private follow-up kind distinguishing current startup candidates from existing queued turns; no new model/runtime API or model-step budget change.\n- Kept the named regression and exercised both authorized and refused third participants; checked every lookup subject and the later turn's authenticated asker.\n- Replaced the fake-only absence-of-decisions assertion with a real-broker audit test using the existing fixture and a positive invocation control, and asserted gateway lookup ownership fields.\n- Updated docs to distinguish consumed steers from final-step leftovers and PR1's behavior from the companion handle change.\n- Retained the worker/race handling and acceptance evidence despite the size estimate: 1,220 added and 60 deleted lines across 7 files.\n- Reopened the draft PR to request CI, then diagnosed the missing pull_request runs as a merge conflict; merged main c22ead28 without rewriting commits and retained both test-module declarations. CI started on the integrated head.\n\n",
